			<font face="Arial" size="-1" color="Black">
<!-- From -->
The official site has received an update in the form of a new <a class=post href=http://www.uo2.com/comments-dev_35.html>Comments from the Team</a> by Knight; here's what he had to say:
<!-- End From -->
<!-- Begin Story -->
<!-- Indent --><table cellspacing="0" cellpadding="2" border="0"><tr><td width="15"><img src="/images/blank.gif" width=15 height=1 border=0 alt=""></td><td><font face="Arial" size="-1" color="#000068"><!-- End Indent-->
Hello all,
<p>

As the Executive Producer on the ORIGIN project, I often get asked what it’s like to work on a development team and, more specifically, what this team is like. Not surprisingly, it all starts with (and ends with!) the people. In my experience, all great teams have three characteristics: Talent, Chemistry and Passion.
<p>

Like sports teams, MMPG development teams require a diverse group of talented individuals. Where those people come from and how they are assembled into a team is more art than science, but hopefully there’s a core group around which to build. "Talent" is a hard thing to measure in the abstract, but it’s readily apparent in the work product. In this case, the talent required to build a MMP project like ours includes:
<p>

Designers knowledgeable about building online worlds and crafting unexploitable systems. 
<p>

Programmers with extensive skills in building clients, servers, networks, scripts, databases, and more. 
<p>

Artists talented enough to generate amazing art within the constraints of an online world. 
<p>

Clearly, MMPGs are massive projects that require a roster of talented people. But just having talent is not enough. A successful team also needs good chemistry. What is "Chemistry?" Fundamentally, it’s the way that the dev team interacts with itself, the company, and the fans. For example, a reclusive team may not be very good at communicating (either internally or externally), whereas a highly social team may have trouble keeping people on task. Chemistry is slowly built over time as the group is confronted by challenges. In fact, each challenge could be seen as an opportunity to define the team’s personality (e.g., how they tackle the inevitable problems which crop up).
<p>

Third on the list is "Passion," but it’s the most important ingredient. Excellence only comes from the things we love to do, and a team without passion is destined to make a mediocre game at best (and one which may never be finished). So, what is passion and how do you get it? Passion is an individual’s personal love for what they are doing. It’s the difference between working on an assembly line and being a craftsman. Passion comes from the opportunity to place a bit of yourself into what you are making. It is that "autonomy of personalization" that is the key in my mind. Conversely, the fastest way to kill passion is to make people feel that they are automatons. Does that really happen? I mean, how can anyone not be passionate about making a game? Actually, there are a lot of different reasons this can happen, but some of the most common in the industry are: (1) getting "reassigned" to the game, (2) not liking the game style or fiction, or (3) seeing a favorite feature cut. In fact, the act of running a team is basically about trying to keep the folks on the project excited about making the game. 
<p>

So, how does our team rank? I am somewhat biased, of course! But I feel that we have some of the best and brightest people in the entire industry. The team has experienced talent from just about every MMP out there, and they are dedicated and tireless. Moreover, they work well together and believe in the game we’re making. They don’t take our fans for granted and they care about communication. I have the greatest respect for the members of this team and it is truly a pleasure to work with them.
<p>

Until next time,
<p>

Jeffrey "Knight" Anderson<br>
VP, Executive in Charge of Production
<!-- /Indent --></font></td></tr></table><!-- End /Indent -->
<!-- End Story -->
<!-- Thanks -->
Thanks!
<!-- End Thanks -->
</font>


