<!-- From -->
Lyria's great at getting details concerning the sky out of Hyperion so here's the latest set of information she's been able to coax from him:
<!-- End From -->
<!-- Begin Story -->
<!-- Indent --><table cellspacing="0" cellpadding="2" border="0"><tr><td width="15"><img src="/images/blank.gif" width=15 height=1 border=0 alt=""></td><td><font face="Arial" size="-1" color="#000068"><!-- End Indent-->
<b>Lyria</b>: 1. How soon do you think the star placement will be complete (as in,
the final change has been made)? I'd be happy to know how much more I must
wait before I can start asking constellation-related questions... (I'm
rapidly running out of question material here too!)
<p>
<b>Hyperion</b>: 1. No idea. Definitely before ship.
<p>
<b>Lyria</b>: 2. Has the Sosarian sun got any name? If not, how about naming it
after me? *huge grin*
<p>
<b>Hyperion</b>: 2. I haven't named it yet. See #8.
<p>
<b>Lyria</b>: 3. If I stand on a (tall) mountain, will I be more able to see the
stars than if I was standing on the ground (less atmosphere to disrupt the
incoming starlight and less atmospheric pollution)?
<p>
<b>Hyperion</b>: 3. Probably not.
<p>
<b>Lyria</b>: 4. Will time pass while the servers are down, or will the celestial
objects be found where they were at server down? This question is important,
since it would matter greatly for me when I start trying to predict eclipses
(and convince people that I am the sun GM)...
<p>
<b>Hyperion</b>: 4. Time passes in the Sosarian calendar regardless of whether the
servers are running.
<p>
<b>Lyria</b>: 5. Besides the optical and day/night length effects, are any
variables affected by the celestial bodies? For instance, will any creature
act differently while Felucca is 95+ percent full?
<p>
<b>Hyperion</b>: 5. Not yet determined. This is one of those things we'd like to
do, but I don't know whether we'll have time to implement it.
<p>
<b>Lyria</b>: 6. Is the orbit of Sosaria a circle or an ellipse?
<p>
<b>Hyperion</b>: 6. A circle. Kepler would love it.
<p>
<b>Lyria</b>: 7. Is there any (considered, planned or completed) spell directly
related to the sky, at least fictionally?
<p>
<b>Hyperion</b>: 7. See #5.
<p>
<b>Lyria</b>: 8. How come you chose the name Hyperion? Was it the figure in Roman
mythology, the famous moon of Saturn (it's famous because it's the largest
known irregularly shaped satellite) or something else?
<p>
<b>Hyperion</b>: 8. From Greek mythology, Hyperion is the Titan of light, the
father of the sun, the moon, and the dawn. I thought it appropriate new
alias for my work in Origin, since I've done all the lighting and skies.
<hr>
<b>Lyria</b>: 1. If I look at the water during the night, will I be able to see
reflections of the celestial objects (assuming that I have a powerful enough
computer)?
<p>
<b>Hyperion</b>: 1. That's the plan. We've yet to implement this with anything
other than test environment maps, so we'll have to see how it goes.
<p>
<b>Lyria</b>: 2. If I somehow got carried away by an invisible monkey to an extreme
height, would the moons and the sun appear larger?
<p>
<b>Hyperion</b>: 2. No. As you speculated correctly in another message, the
celestial objects are rendered differently than the world objects you can
interact with. They always maintain their set virtual distance from the
camera.
<p>
<b>Lyria</b>: 3. Are the orbits of Trammel and Felucca tilted? If yes, do they have
an individual tilt or do they follow the same path across the sky (just at
different paces due to the difference in distance)? One of the reasons for
me to ask this, is that it would affect the chances of the different kinds
of solar eclipses to take place...
<p>
<b>Hyperion</b>: 3. Don't you want to figure these out by observation? Both orbits
are tilted from the equatorial plane. Felucca's orbit is tilted by 5 degrees
towards a sidereal azimuth of 330 degrees. Trammel's is tilted 8 degrees
towards an azimuth of 120 degrees. These values are arbitrary.
<p>
<b>Lyria</b>: 5. Will we be able to somehow tell what date it is in-game? This is
something that I will need to be able to do in order to verify when a
celestial event will take place in Real Life time.
<p>
<b>Hyperion</b>: 5. Yes, you will have an in-game clock/calendar.
<p>
<b>Lyria</b>: 6. Will all the shards be of the same in-game date at the same time,
or will they follow different calendars?
<p>
<b>Hyperion</b>: 6. All shards should follow the same calendar.
<p>
<b>Lyria</b>: 7. What are the chances of convincing the staff to delay server
maintenance if I discover that a really rare and spectacular celestial
happening is about to take place during scheduled downtime? Or at least, to
convince the live team to turn back (calendar) time somehow?
<p>
<b>Hyperion</b>: 7. Some extremely small non-zero number. Effectively, none. I'm
fairly sure the live team will be aware of when spectacular celestial events
will occur and plan accordingly.
<p>
<b>Lyria</b>: 8. How large are the moons when compared to the sun? For instance,
are we going to be able to experience total solar eclipses with Felucca and
circular solar eclipses with Trammel?
<p>
<b>Hyperion</b>: 8. Both moons have a larger arc radius than the sun.
<p>
<b>Lyria</b>: 9. Are the orbits of Trammel and Felucca circular or elliptical? I'd
assume circular, but I can't let a thing like that slip away.
<p>
<b>Hyperion</b>: 9. Circular orbits. Easy.
<p>
<b>Lyria</b>: 11. Considering the... disposition... of some of the development team
members, how likely is it that we are going to see the celestial
constellations of 'the monkey' and 'the banana'?
<p>
<b>Hyperion</b>: 11. The funny thing about constellations is that everyone sees
something different. The Greeks saw their gods. The Chinese saw dragons and
emperors. The Arabs saw their cultural heros. Europeans in the middle ages
saw anvils and cookware. Maybe we'll see monkeys and bananas.
<!-- /Indent --></font></td></tr></table><!-- End /Indent -->
<!-- End Story -->
<!-- Thanks -->
Thanks!
<!-- End Thanks -->
</font>


