			<font face="Arial" size="-1" color="Black">
<!-- From -->
From the <a class=post href=http://boards.uo2.com/>Official Boards</a>:
<!-- End From -->
<!-- Begin Story -->
<!-- Indent --><table cellspacing="0" cellpadding="2" border="0"><tr><td width="15"><img src="/images/blank.gif" width=15 height=1 border=0 alt=""></td><td><font face="Arial" size="-1" color="#000068"><!-- End Indent-->
<b>Environment Mapped Bump Mapping</b><br>For the unwashed: EMBM is Environment Mapped Bump Mapping.<br><br>We're actually trying to skip past EMBM for more advanced hardware. EMBM is somewhat of a hack (in the pejurative sense); there are better ways of doing this type of effect on newer hardware. I'm referring to what Microsoft calls "pixel shaders" which aren't available on any current hardware, but should be part of the feature set for every next generation video card from the major players at the time we RTM.<br><br>What pixel shaders give us is the ability to do per pixel lighting, bump-mapping, reflection mapping, and other cool stuff that graphics programmers have been dreaming of for years. We've been planning on using these advanced hardware features for quite some time; it's rather tricky, though, when the hardware isn't available yet.<br><br><b><i>Hyperion</i></b><p><b>Remapping Keys</b><br>The keys are complete remappable and you can use a combination of the keyboard and the mouse. If you like arrow keys, you can use arrow keys. If you like WASD, you can use those. You could map it like U9 if you like that.<br><br>Our mouse/keyboard combo is pretty intuitive; most of us are using the mouse to look around (click and drag) and the WASD keys to move. These may end up as the default, or we might come up with something even better. We've got a lot of time set aside to test this sort of thing and we'll be getting feedback from thousands of testers.<br><br><b><i>Hyperion</i></b><p><b>Quick Keys</b><br>I'm not sure about the macro capability - I'd have to defer to one of the UI guys like Medwynd.<br><br>I know that you will be able to assign your favorite spells or combat moves to hot keys or quick slots.<br><br><b><i>Hyperion</i></b><p><b>Spell Effects and Lag</b><br>Both shren and Sparrowhawk are correct.<br><br>Spell effects require very little bandwidth. Basically, a server will tell your client, "gob #5813247 just had fluff package #583 attached at connection point #33". Pretty simple stuff to transmit.<br><br>Lag is caused by delays in network traffic. This can be a result of high traffic at our end (which we've done a lot of work to avoid), or, more likely, high traffic somewhere along the route to your computer, e.g., when a fellow ISP subscriber is downloading an MPEG movie off a [censored] site. There's not much we can do about the latter except to make our clients a little smarter so they can *pretend* there's no lag.<br><br><b><i>Hyperion</i></b><p><b>Dynamic Lighting</b><br>Yes, we support dynamic lighting. Heck, it's the only kind of lighting we do. And the spells are looking damned good.<br><br>It's too early speculate on system specs. We should have a better idea once we're done with our optimization phase. We'll let you know with an update to our FAQ.<br><br><b><i>Hyperion</i></b><p><b>DX8</b><br>Origin requires DX8, therefore it will run on Windows 2000(NT5) or later, but not on earlier versions of NT. <br><br>SkiCat<p><b>Terrain Changes</b><br>We're trying to steer away from terrain changes. While it's something we can do (its just a client data patch), our terrain data is higher detail than AC, and swapping in new terrain for a patch could cause some pretty hefty downloading.<br><br>We're looking at some other pretty cool ways of modifying the game experience for events. We've got some nifty stuff planned (wink).<br><br><b><i>Hyperion</i></b><p><b>Size of Install</b><br>We're aiming towards 2 CDs, you can install one and run with the other in the drive, or install both and run off your HD.  Er, that'd be around 650M footprint for one or 1300M for both.  This is mega subject to change by ship, however, but it's what we're targetting for.  We may or may not allow that footprint to grow or shrink based on whether or not we uncompress stuff pre-loading for time savings at a HD footprint cost.  Also, we may or may not get it all done and say "dang, we need 47 DVDs" but our projections and current footprints are saying 2 CDs is the probable number, with an likely error margin of a possible 3rd.<br><br>A mega-slow version where we do a minimal install and then you swap CDs in and out is possible but really really unlikely unless lots of people scream loudly.<br><br>-jg<p><b>Concerning the Camera</b><br>You don't always need to see the back of your head.  The camera is not fixed at viewing you from behind.  It can be zoomed in and out as well as rotated around your avatar.<br><br>Medwynd<br>User Interface Programmer<br>UWO: Origin<p><b>First Person View</b><br>I plan on testing a true 1st person POV camera -- true in the sense that it will track with all avatar head movements (even idle animations). Then I'm going to get a good part of the team to watch while I do some dancing, bowing, jumping, and general gesturing. Then I'll do some combat moves: forward and reverse flips, spins, flying kicks, etc.<br><br>Then we'll all vomit. It should be fun.<br><br><b><i>Hyperion</i></b><p><b>Massively Multiplayer Market</b><br>The thing is, the market for these games have barely been touched.  Let's look at America alone.  There are about 100 million American homes that have computers that can handle higher end computer games.  Out of that, there are about 10 million homes that buy computer games on a regular basis.  Out of that there are only about half a million homes that have ever tried an online game.  In otherwords, out of a potential market, PSW games only have .5% of their potential audience.  That's just in the states.  Take it world wide and you see how far we have to go bring people into the online gaming experience.<br><br>My guess is that within 5 years we'll see about 5 to 6 million people playing games online.  Since the market can currently support 3 major online games with a population of half a million players, it stands to reason that a playerbase of 6 million can support 36 large scale games and make them a sucess. (defining "suces" as games that are capable to make a large enough profit to justify the expense.)<br><br>Of course, one has to wonder how many games an individual person can play.  I'm guessing 2, perhaps 3.  I see myself playing UO and UWO:O and leaving some time to try out each game that comes out.<br><br><br>"G." Bob<br>Community Coordinator<br>"Nemo enim fere saltat sobrius, nisi forte insanit." <p><b>Origin On The PS2?</b><br>Not sure where that report came from, but right now we're not considering porting the game over to the PS/2.  Trying to support two different operating systems is a difficult thing.  If we were to patch the game for example, the patch would have to written for both systems....and that would be difficult for any company to manage.<br><br>Mind you, it's always possible for this too change, but we haven't even begun to consider it for our game.<br><br><br>"G." Bob<br>Community Coordinator<br>"Nemo enim fere saltat sobrius, nisi forte insanit." <p><b>Teleportation Spells</b><br>There are currently no teleport spells inside of the game - quick transportation is limited primarily to the moongates.  <br><br>If we are to add such spells in the future, they would be far different in utility and application than what UO has, and be far more limited in how much they can be used (moonglobes are great for that).<p><b>Wind</b><br>Yes, there is wind. Of course, it's invisible. *smirk*<br><br>You'll be able to see the effect of wind on particle effects, like fire, smoke, dust, etc. You'll be able to see the smoke from the stacks in Logos drifting away with the wind. This is all working now.<br><br>We're also looking at having wind materially effect some objects like banners, signs, and flags, and possible water. We're working on this last part now, so nothing definite yet.<br><br><b><i>Hyperion</i></b><p><b>More on Wind</b><br>The plan is for wind to be a client-side only effect. This would mean no effect on game play, which eliminates a lot of potential work and complication for the server.<br><br><b><i>Hyperion</i></b><p><b>Line of Sight</b><br>There is an LOS for archers (and 99.99 percent of offensive spells).<br><br>Sitting where a monster can't hit you and killing it with no danger is Bad.<br><br>-jg<p><b>Level of Detail and Polys</b><br>The Jukan armor model has 484 polys at its highest level-of-detail.  That is a pretty good estimate for the majority of character models' poly counts.<br><br>--LevelDevil<p><b>Extending LOD</b><br>We've built in the ability to extend the detail on the models at any time. We fully anticipate that the typical machine 2 years from will will be able to handle an order of magnitude more geometry than we are planning in the initial release.<br><br>We're also targetting above what we anticipate a typical machine is now and relying on our LOD system to automatically scale back. As hardware performance increases, the system should automatically take advantage of improved framerate to start adding more detail.<br><br><b><i>Hyperion</i></b><p><b>Polys and the Yew Tree</b><br>There are not as many polys on the Yew tree as you might think.  It is just that the polys are very big.  Also, there is a lot of visual trickery going on.  Each level of the tree only has somewhere around 13,000 polys, but since it is so big, you only really see a couple thousand from any given place.<br><br>--LevelDevil<p><b>Inventory Slots</b><br>Two notes:<br>1) The twenty-four inventory slots does not include the 'worn items'/paper doll slots.  They are in addition to them.  24 is purely carried items - i.e. things in your backpack.<br>2) 24 is a working number, and may well change before we ship.  It's just what we're working with for now.  Allowing lots of carried items has a detrimental effect on database size, which results in things like long bootup times.  On the other hand, the crafting-oriented bent of the game demands that players can hold a lot of tools and resources, so we gotta find what is the best balance.<br><br>Ubiq<p><b>Rippling Waves</b><br>We don't know for sure yet. The water and lava that you've seen so far is purely procedural. That is to say that the waves exist and we can set the wave frequency, number of waves, their direction, the liquid viscosity, and other parameters, but they do not react to objects moving in them. We would like to add dynamic "ripple" waves if we have time, but there is no guarantee at this point, just like any other "eye-candy" feature. <br><br>As is the case with many advanced features, there are also performance and design considerations. For example, will players on low-end machines also see the ripples? If they don't, does this adversely affect that player's gaming experience? <br><br>But hang in there, we're not quite done yet!<br><br><br>SkiCat<p><b>On Waves</b><br>The grid that makes up water and lava is dynamically changeable based on the size of the waves and the performance of the machine it's running on. This way we can crank it up as high as needed to make it look smooth, but it doesn't really need to be much smaller than half the shortest wavelength wave in the wave field to look OK.<br><br>One interesting note, when I was implementing the wave fields I tried cranking up the ocean so that there were millions of triangles drawing per second at good framerates on a GeForce II. We don't need to do that in practice, but shows the capability of some of the high end cards available today.<br><br><br>SkiCat<p><b>Concerning Wielding Two Handed Weapons With One Hand</b><br>Nope, not as things stand now.  I'm looking at allowing some 2-handed weapons to be weilded one-handed as well, but no promises (for instance, katanas or suchlike).  If it falls cleanly out of the design, cool, but if it requires major hackery (lots of different code in places) then it won't be worth it.<br><br>-jg<p><b>Concerning Attacks</b><br>Not just ranged attacks but also melee attacks can have spell-like effects associated with them.  Also, there are "ranged melee" attacks (like the corkscrew kick and flying side kick - that's what the fella in the recent screenshot is caught in the middle of) which move you into combat range before doing the damage (and often, then back out of of combat range).  This is handy since these attacks don't work well with armor, so you don't want to be going toe-to-toe...they're powerful, but if you take a hit while in close range, you'll be in trouble.<br><br>Since archers don't even expose themselves to even this much danger, their attacks tend to be less powerful, though at the higher levels still interesting.  The more powerful the archer attacks, the more armor limitations are in place.<br><br>Balanced but not equal, risk equals reward - tha usual drill :)<br><br>-jg<p><b>The Look of GMs</b><br>There has actually been a good amount of talk on how to make GM effects look cool.  As with any brainstorming sessions, most ideas are just too silly to put in the game. :)<br><br>To be honest, we haven't decided yet.<br><br><br>"G." Bob<br>Community Coordinator<br>"Nemo enim fere saltat sobrius, nisi forte insanit." <p><b>Third Party Programs</b><br>Fundamentaly, there are three ways to prevent the use of third party programs.<br><br>The first is active enforcement.  One of the big changes in UO over the past year was better use of the support staff when it comes to enforceing the game rules.<br><br>Next is better security and encryption on the game itself.<br><br>The most import factor, however, is to eliminate the need for "cheat" programs.  This is really the most important one, and if done properly eliniates the need for the other two.  In many games "cheat" programs are used because they offer features that people really want in the game.  The better tyhe dev team does their job, the less need there is for these kinds of programs.<br><br><br><br>"G." Bob<br>Community Coordinator<br>"Nemo enim fere saltat sobrius, nisi forte insanit."
<!-- /Indent --></font></td></tr></table><!-- End /Indent -->
<!-- End Story -->
<!-- Thanks -->
Thanks!
<!-- End Thanks -->
</font>


