			<font face="Arial" size="-1" color="Black">
<!-- From -->

<!-- End From -->
<!-- Begin Story -->
Although the rapid rate of progress within the industry sometimes makes it feel like it happened at least a few years earlier, Ultima Online was actually released commercially by Origin Systems in 1997.  Other games can lay claim to being the first persistent online world, but there can be no real doubt that UO was the landmark title that very dramatically ushered in a new era for the entire computer gaming industry.  It demonstrated not only that “massively multiplayer” gaming was feasible with unprecedented numbers of players, but also that the subscription-based revenue model was practicable, and thus that it could be hugely successful, with unprecedented numbers of subscribers. 
<p>
Last autumn, the company announced an exciting follow-up title, thereby becoming the first developer to reveal plans for a second generation online world.  Initially referred to as Ultima Online 2, <a class=post href="http://www.uo2.com/" target="_new">Ultima Worlds Online: Origin</a> also promises to set new standards for the genre with a plethora of innovations and features.  Among these is a strong backstory that incorporates a new Ultima world on a divergent timeline and two new races, the technologically-oriented Juka and the magically-inclined Meer, along with humans.  Recently, we had the chance to learn more about this aspect of the game in an interesting and informative <a class=post href=" http://uo2vault.ign.com/ahrimaninterview.asp" target="_new">Ultima Worlds Online: Origin Interview</a> with its Minister of Fiction, Aaron de Orive, also known as Ahriman.
<!-- End Story -->
