<!-- From -->
The Dev's over Thanksgiving have posted a few things over on the <a class=post href=http://boards.stratics.com/boards/origin/index.html>Stratics DevBoard</a>; here's a rundown:
<!-- End From -->
<!-- Begin Story -->
<!-- Indent --><table cellspacing="0" cellpadding="2" border="0"><tr><td width="15"><img src="/images/blank.gif" width=15 height=1 border=0 alt=""></td><td><font face="Arial" size="-1" color="#000068"><!-- End Indent-->
<b>Pre-Placed Housing</b><br><i>How about the Design Team puts the houses in the game rather than letting us place them. We just have to buy one that is already placed and recieve the master key. Once we own it, it acts just like a house from UO except that if upkeep is not maintained, then the house is not destroyed but rather placed back on the market.</i><br><br>This is what I did in GemStone III and DragonRealms (a couple of good text MUDS, try 'em on Microsoft's Game Zone) when I put houses in those games.<br><br>It actually was a lot of fun for the players; we had the empty houses on display for a while before we let people take them, and people got really attached to specific homes in specific locations.  If you didn't get the one you wanted right away, you always knew that someday (if you were lucky) it would be empty again.<br><br>And yeah, it did limit the clutter, which is pretty important in a text game :)<br><br>Windfeather<br>Designer o' Stuff<br>That Ultima Game<br><br><i>Real life is the ultimate roleplaying game.</i><p><b>Intelligent Creatures and Spawn</b><br><i>I am all for unique NPCs and monsters, but not if it means using static spawns and camping. All spawns should be pretty much random. I wouldn't mind "Nefir Nefarious the Infamous Pirate" spawning anywhere in the world by the shore, but I would mind if he spawned in the same exact place every time and there was a group of people lined up to take turns killing him.</i><br><br>For the most part, intelligent monsters (Orcs, Ettins, Snipes, Foozles, etc) have their own regions on the map where they are the prevalent thing to hunt.  Thus, when walking about you'd know you were in 'Goblin Territory', 'Orc Territory', etc.<br><br>Within those regions you will find special sites - graveyards, forts, gigantic shoes with old women in them - where you can hang out and kill more concentrated, and tougher, groups of monsters.<br><br>Fauna (critters) will spawn according to the geography.  Rats in swamps, artists in coffee shops, executives on golf courses.  That kind of thing.<br><br>There won't be any static spawns of 'named creature X' showing up to be killed repeatedly; however, you may find unique areas that contain the only spawn for really hard monsters such as Undead Orc Assassin Ninjas.<br><br>Windfeather<br>Designer o' Stuff<br>That Ultima Game<br><br><i>Real life is the ultimate roleplaying game.</i><p><b>Starting Cities</b><br><i>Will you be able to start ANYWHERE in one of origin's city's OR will you have to pick a city within your selected races' Province ?¿?</i><br><br>There are currently five starting cities, all of them in safe zones (since there's not much to do in the Wild Lands as a newbie except cause trouble).  Three of the cities are Britannian in flavor, one Meer and one Jukan.  You may start in any of them, regardless of your race.<br><br>Windfeather<br>Designer o' Stuff<br>That Ultima Game<br><br><i>Real life is the ultimate roleplaying game.</i><p><b>Death Animations</b><br>You can die in lots of different ways, actually - there are a number of death/knockdown animations. We didn't want to show em off just now in the video (they haven't all been fully beautified)
<p>
-jg
<p><b>Grief Players</b><br>You misunderstand.<br><br>The commonly accepted definition of a grief player is a player who plays to ruin the good time of other people for no good reason.  Grief players are generally considered bad, and the game design needs to try to account for them.  Player Killers (PKers) are a subset of grief players, but you don't need to be able to kill someone to cause grief - you can cyberrape them, train monsters on them, steal their loot, etc, etc, etc, etc.<br><br>Players who take part in consentual PvP because they are at war with another guild are not considered Grief Players.  They are considered PvPers.  Game designers like PvP.  We wish more people would take part in consentual PvP.  PvP can be the very lifeblood of the game.<br><br>In short, the term 'grief player' is a shorthand way to refer to troublesome elements in the game, and does not include everyone who engages in player-killing.  I would go so far to venture that a fraction of UO's player killers are actually grief players (of course, where that line is is hard to define since it varies from person to person).<br><br>Ubiq
<!-- /Indent --></font></td></tr></table><!-- End /Indent -->
<!-- End Story -->
<!-- Thanks -->
Thanks!
<!-- End Thanks -->
</font>


